}
```

Creating a chat app using Transformers.js and the Facebook/BlenderBot-400M-Distill model in pure vanilla JavaScript involves several steps. Here's a step-by-step guide to help you get started:

**Step 1: Set up the environment**

Create a new HTML file (e.g., `index.html`) and add a script tag to include the Transformers.js library. You can use a CDN or download the library and host it yourself.
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Chat App</title>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@tensorflow/tfjs@3.13.0/dist/tf.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@tensorflow-models/universal-sentence-encoder@1.0.2/dist/universal-sentence-encoder.min.js"></script>
    <script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/transformers@4.10.1/dist/transformers.min.js"></script>
</head>
<body>
    <!-- Chat app UI will be rendered here -->
    <div id="chat-app"></div>
    <script src="script.js"></script>
</body>
</html>
```
**Step 2: Load the BlenderBot-400M-Distill model**

Create a new JavaScript file (e.g., `script.js`) and load the BlenderBot-400M-Distill model using the `transformers` library.
```javascript
// Import necessary libraries
import * as tf from '@tensorflow/tfjs';
import { UniversalSentenceEncoder } from '@tensorflow-models/universal-sentence-encoder';
import { BlenderBot400MDistill } from 'transformers';

// Load the BlenderBot-400M-Distill model
const model = await BlenderBot400MDistill.fromPreTrained('facebook/blenderbot-400m-distill');
```
**Step 3: Set up the chat app UI**

Create a basic chat app UI using HTML and CSS. You can add a text input field, a send button, and a chat log container.
```html
<!-- Chat app UI -->
<div id="chat-app">
    <input id="user-input" type="text" placeholder="Type a message...">
    <button id="send-button">Send</button>
    <div id="chat-log"></div>
</div>
```
**Step 4: Handle user input and send messages**

Add an event listener to the send button to handle user input and send messages to the model.
```javascript
// Get the user input field and send button
const userInputField = document.getElementById('user-input');
const sendButton = document.getElementById('send-button');

// Add an event listener to the send button
sendButton.addEventListener('click', async () => {
    // Get the user input
    const userInput = userInputField.value.trim();

    // Send the user input to the model
    const response = await model.generate(userInput);

    // Render the response in the chat log
    renderResponse(response);
});
```
**Step 5: Render responses in the chat log**

Create a function to render responses in the chat log container.
```javascript
// Render a response in the chat log
function renderResponse(response) {
    // Get the chat log container
    const chatLog = document.getElementById('chat-log');

    // Create a new chat log message element
    const messageElement = document.createElement('div');
    messageElement.textContent = response;

    // Add the message element to the chat log container
    chatLog.appendChild(messageElement);
}
```
**Step 6: Handle model responses**

Modify the `renderResponse` function to handle model responses.
```javascript
// Render a response in the chat log
function renderResponse(response) {
    // Get the chat log container
    const chatLog = document.getElementById('chat-log');

    // Create a new chat log message element
    const messageElement = document.createElement('div');
    messageElement.textContent = response.generated_text;

    // Add the message element to the chat log container
    chatLog.appendChild(messageElement);
}
```
That's it! You now have a basic chat app using Transformers.js and the Facebook/BlenderBot-400M-Distill model in pure vanilla JavaScript.

