    <groupId>io.kubernetes</groupId>
    <artifactId>client-java</artifactId>
    <version>15.0.1</version>
</dependency>
```

Gradle:
```groovy
implementation 'io.kubernetes:client-java:15.0.1'
```

### Step 2: Create a Kubernetes Configuration

Create a `KubernetesConfig` class to store the Kubernetes configuration. This class will hold the API server URL, namespace, and authentication token.

```java
public class KubernetesConfig {
    private String apiServerUrl;
    private String namespace;
    private String authToken;

    // Getters and setters
    public String getApiServerUrl() {
        return apiServerUrl;
    }

    public void setApiServerUrl(String apiServerUrl) {
        this.apiServerUrl = apiServerUrl;
    }

    public String getNamespace() {
        return namespace;
    }

    public void setNamespace(String namespace) {
        this.namespace = namespace;
    }

    public String getAuthToken() {
        return authToken;
    }

    public void setAuthToken(String authToken) {
        this.authToken = authToken;
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Create a Kubernetes Client

Create a `KubernetesClient` class to handle the communication with the Kubernetes API server. This class will use the Java HTTP Client to send requests to the Kubernetes API server.

```java
import java.io.IOException;
import java.net.URI;
import java.net.http.HttpClient;
import java.net.http.HttpRequest;
import java.net.http.HttpResponse;

public class KubernetesClient {
    private KubernetesConfig config;
    private HttpClient httpClient;

    public KubernetesClient(KubernetesConfig config) {
        this.config = config;
        this.httpClient = HttpClient.newHttpClient();
    }

    public String getPodLogs(String podName) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        // Create the API request
        String url = String.format("%s/api/v1/namespaces/%s/pods/%s/log", config.getApiServerUrl(), config.getNamespace(), podName);
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
                .uri(URI.create(url))
                .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + config.getAuthToken())
                .GET()
                .build();

        // Send the request and get the response
        HttpResponse<String> response = httpClient.send(request, HttpResponse.BodyHandlers.ofString());

        // Check the response status code
        if (response.statusCode() != 200) {
            throw new IOException("Failed to get pod logs: " + response.statusCode());
        }

        // Return the pod logs
        return response.body();
    }
}
```

### Step 4: Use the Kubernetes Client

Create a `Main` class to demonstrate how to use the `KubernetesClient` class to read pod logs.

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException, InterruptedException {
        // Create a Kubernetes configuration
        KubernetesConfig config = new KubernetesConfig();
        config.setApiServerUrl("https://your-kubernetes-api-server.com");
        config.setNamespace("your-namespace");
        config.setAuthToken("your-auth-token");

        // Create a Kubernetes client
        KubernetesClient client = new KubernetesClient(config);

        // Get the pod logs
        String podName = "your-pod-name";
        String logs = client.getPodLogs(podName);

        // Print the pod logs
        System.out.println(logs);
    }
}
```

Replace the placeholders (`your-kubernetes-api-server.com`, `your-namespace`, `your-auth-token`, and `your-pod-name`) with your actual Kubernetes API server URL, namespace, authentication token, and pod name.

