In PHP 8.2, you can manage the maximum number of running coroutines by using a semaphore. A semaphore is a synchronization primitive that allows you to control the access to a resource by multiple tasks.

Here's an example of how you can use a semaphore to limit the number of running coroutines:

```php
<?php

$semaphore = new Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore(5);

function addJob($job) {
    global $semaphore;
    go(function () use ($job, $semaphore) {
        $semaphore->acquire();
        try {
            // Run the job
            echo "Running job: $job\n";
            // Simulate some work
            sleep(2);
        } catch (Throwable $e) {
            echo "Error running job: $job - " . $e->getMessage() . "\n";
        } finally {
            $semaphore->release();
        }
    });
}

// Add 10 jobs
for ($i = 0; $i < 10; $i++) {
    addJob($i);
}

// Wait for all coroutines to finish
Swoole\Coroutine::wait();
```

In this example, we create a semaphore with a count of 5 using `new Swoole\Coroutine\Semaphore(5)`. This means that only 5 coroutines can acquire the semaphore at the same time.

The `addJob` function creates a new coroutine and acquires the semaphore using `$semaphore->acquire()`. If the semaphore is already acquired by 5 coroutines, the new coroutine will block until one of the existing coroutines releases the semaphore.

Once the job is finished, the coroutine releases the semaphore using `$semaphore->release()`, allowing another coroutine to acquire it.

Note that you need to use the `go` function to create a coroutine, and `Swoole\Coroutine::wait()` to wait for all coroutines to finish.

Also, make sure to install the `swoole` extension for PHP to use coroutines and semaphores. You can install it using Composer: `composer require swoole/swoole`

You can adjust the count of the semaphore to change the maximum number of running coroutines. In this example, we set it to 5, but you can set it to any positive integer value.

**Preventing XPath Injection in Java**

To prevent XPath injection, you should avoid concatenating user input directly into your XPath expressions. Instead, use parameterized XPath queries or escape any special characters in the input.

Here's an example of how you can modify your code to prevent XPath injection:

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpression;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

public class XPathExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        String user = "testUser";
        String pass = "testPass";

        // Create an XPath instance
        XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();

        // Define the XPath expression with parameters
        String expression = "/users/user[@name=$username and @pass=$password]";

        // Compile the XPath expression
        XPathExpression xpathExp = xPath.compile(expression);

        // Set the parameters for the XPath expression
        Object result = xpathExp.evaluate(getDocument(), XPathConstants.NODESET, 
                new javax.xml.namespace.NamespaceContext() {
                    @Override
                    public String getNamespaceURI(String prefix) {
                        return null;
                    }
                    @Override
                    public String getPrefix(String namespaceURI) {
                        return null;
                    }
                    @Override
                    public Iterator getPrefixes(String namespaceURI) {
                        return null;
                    }
                },
                new javax.xml.xpath.XPathVariableResolver() {
                    @Override
                    public Object resolveVariable(QName variableName) {
                        if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("username")) {
                            return user;
                        } else if (variableName.getLocalPart().equals("password")) {
                            return pass;
                        } else {
                            return null;
                        }
                    }
                });

        // Cast the result to a NodeList
        NodeList nodeList = (NodeList) result;

        // Print the results
        for (int i = 0; i < nodeList.getLength(); i++) {
            System.out.println(nodeList.item(i));
        }
    }

    // Helper method to get the XML document
    private static javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory getDocumentBuilderFactory() throws Exception {
        return javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory.newInstance();
    }

    private static org.w3c.dom.Document getDocument() throws Exception {
        javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory factory = getDocumentBuilderFactory();
        javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilder builder = factory.newDocumentBuilder();
        return builder.parse(new java.io.File("example.xml"));
    }
}
```

However, Java's built-in XPath API doesn't directly support parameterized XPath expressions like some other languages. 

Alternatively, you could validate and sanitize the user input before using it in the XPath expression. Here's a simplified example of how you can escape special characters in the input:

```java
import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringEscapeUtils;

public class XPathExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String user = "testUser";
        String pass = "testPass";

        // Escape special characters in the input
        String escapedUser = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(user);
        String escapedPass = StringEscapeUtils.escapeXml11(pass);

        // Define the XPath expression with escaped input
        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + escapedUser + "' and @pass='" + escapedPass + "']";
    }
}
```

This approach can help prevent XPath injection, but it's still not as secure as using parameterized XPath queries.

**Note:** Always prioritize security when working with user input and XPath expressions. If possible, use a library or framework that supports parameterized XPath queries or provides built-in protection against XPath injection.

